<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afonso I of Kongo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1509">1509</a>–<a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Afonso I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afonso I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, also known as Nzinga Mbemba, was king of the Kongo Empire from 1509 to 1543. A committed Catholic, he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, literacy and ties with Portugal, while trying to strengthen royal authority. His surviving letters to the Portuguese crown protest the damage caused by the growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo.jpg" alt="Afonso I of Kongo" width="293" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Affricoll9 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manikongo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Loango is absent from the titles of King <b>Afonso I of Kongo</b> in 1535.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1518 the ngola sent envoys to Portugal asking for missionaries, but the mission that came in 1520 achieved little, and <b>Afonso I of Kongo</b> took its priests to his own court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom expanded and held together by campaigning, particularly during the reign of <b>Afonso I</b> (r. 1509–1543), who won the throne by defeating his half-brother Mpanzu a Kitima and then sought to strengthen central authority and suppress rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Afonso I</b>, king from 1509, made Catholicism the royal faith, nobles learned to read in royal schools and took Portuguese names and, later, titles such as duke and count.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kongo">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Kongo</a></li><li><blockquote>With crusader help, <b>Afonso I</b>, the first king of Portugal, ended a siege begun on 1 July and took Lisbon from the Moors.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-25">25 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It stayed under Badajoz until 1093, was briefly ceded to Alfonso VI of Castile, then passed to the Almoravids, and <b>Afonso I</b> of the Kingdom of Portugal took it in 1147 with the help of crusaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-lisbon">Taifa of Lisbon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">M'banza-Kongo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">M'banza-Kongo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-6.27,14.25&amp;year=1526" aria-label="M'banza-Kongo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4945 2398 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Afonso I of Kongo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5395 2668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5395 2668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-6.27,14.25&amp;year=1526">What was here in 1526? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
